From 92c3fcd035d9c49ebbf43f7eeb5ed1d5ea5edaa6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 09:55:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Move the create/disable toggle out of Advanced options, into
 the form footer

- Removed the per-type 'Create disabled' checkbox from every Advanced
  options accordion (it was the last remaining field there for
  redirect/custom, which now just show the 'No advanced options for
  this block type yet.' message in both new and edit mode).
- Added one shared toggle switch after the Logging accordion, used by
  all five block types alike: labeled 'Create disabled' when creating
  a new block, 'Disable this block' when editing an existing one.
- The edit form's toggle reflects the block's current .disabled state
  and, on Save, applies whatever state the user leaves it in --
  replacing the separate header Enable/Disable button (removed) and
  its standalone POST, for that page specifically. Added
  set_block_disabled() in blocks.py (renames the .conf + metadata
  sidecar to add/remove the suffix, no-op if already matching) and
  refactored the existing /toggle route (used by the site-blocks
  list's dropdown action, which is unchanged) to share it, so the two
  entry points can't drift.
- Renamed the field from create_disabled to disabled (one name for
  both modes) and the input's id from create-disabled-input to
  block-disabled-input, updating the one line of JS (the live
  conf-path-badge preview) that reads it by id.

Verified: all five block types' new-block pages show the toggle once,
after Logging and before Save, with the old checkbox and field name
gone; redirect/custom's Advanced options now read 'No advanced options'
in both modes; creating with the toggle on writes a .disabled file;
editing to flip the toggle either way renames the .conf (and sidecar)
on save; the old /toggle route (site-blocks list) still works via the
shared helper; a jsdom check confirms the live conf-path badge still
picks up the toggle's checked state under its new id. Full prior
regression suite (logging create/edit/rename/delete, rotation, fallback
parsing) still passes. pyflakes clean.
